1
00:00:00,859 --> 00:00:02,560
In the year 1200,

2
00:00:02,580 --> 00:00:04,850
almost nobody had heard of the Mongols.

3
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:06,290
Eighty years later,

4
00:00:06,310 --> 00:00:09,480
they ruled the largest connected empire in history.

5
00:00:10,290 --> 00:00:12,190
If the year 1200 had a crown,

6
00:00:12,210 --> 00:00:13,440
China would wear it.

7
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:15,600
It was the richest place on Earth,

8
00:00:15,650 --> 00:00:17,862
with paper money, printing, gunpowder,

9
00:00:17,882 --> 00:00:20,100
and cities of a million people.

10
00:00:21,110 --> 00:00:22,810
Far to the north lay the steppe:

11
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:24,400
an ocean of grass.

12
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:26,230
Here lived the Mongols.

13
00:00:26,380 --> 00:00:27,477
Maybe a million people,

14
00:00:27,497 --> 00:00:29,490
with no cities and no writing.

15
00:00:29,570 --> 00:00:31,850
Just tents, sheep, and horses.

16
00:00:31,980 --> 00:00:34,520
And their clans fought each other all the time.

17
00:00:35,590 --> 00:00:39,220
Around 1162, a boy named Temüjin was born here.

18
00:00:39,420 --> 00:00:41,830
When he was nine, his father was poisoned.

19
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:44,550
His clan left the family alone in the cold.

20
00:00:44,710 --> 00:00:47,340
Thieves once stole eight of their nine horses.

21
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:48,906
Temüjin was even captured,

22
00:00:48,926 --> 00:00:50,930
and locked in a heavy wooden collar.

23
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:52,400
He escaped.

24
00:00:53,160 --> 00:00:56,293
Step by step, with friends, deals, and battles,

25
00:00:56,313 --> 00:00:58,190
he united the tribes of the steppe.

26
00:00:58,410 --> 00:01:00,940
In 1206, they gave him a new title:

27
00:01:01,090 --> 00:01:02,330
Genghis Khan.

28
00:01:03,380 --> 00:01:05,690
Then he looked at the rich world around him.

29
00:01:05,980 --> 00:01:08,565
So how could a small people with no cities

30
00:01:08,585 --> 00:01:10,700
beat the greatest empires on Earth?

31
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:12,570
Here are five reasons.

32
00:01:13,450 --> 00:01:15,180
One: horses.

33
00:01:15,430 --> 00:01:17,156
Every rider brought several horses,

34
00:01:17,176 --> 00:01:19,950
and jumped onto a fresh one when the first got tired.

35
00:01:20,140 --> 00:01:23,390
Mongol armies moved faster than any army of their time.

36
00:01:23,450 --> 00:01:25,940
And their riders could shoot arrows at full gallop.

37
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:27,100
Even backwards.

38
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:30,210
Two: a new kind of army.

39
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:32,600
Genghis broke the old clans apart.

40
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:34,361
Soldiers were put into groups of ten,

41
00:01:34,381 --> 00:01:37,250
a hundred, a thousand, and ten thousand.

42
00:01:37,430 --> 00:01:40,090
Leaders were chosen for skill, not for family.

43
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:42,280
Subutai, the son of a blacksmith,

44
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:44,740
became one of the greatest generals in history.

45
00:01:45,720 --> 00:01:47,540
Three: information.

46
00:01:47,740 --> 00:01:49,240
The Mongols built the Yam,

47
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:51,460
a chain of stations with fresh horses.

48
00:01:51,580 --> 00:01:54,900
A messenger could ride around two hundred kilometers in a day.

49
00:01:55,020 --> 00:01:58,420
And spies and traders told the khan everything about his enemies,

50
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:00,090
long before the first battle.

51
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,180
Four: they learned fast.

52
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:06,170
At first, the Mongols could not take walled cities.

53
00:02:06,190 --> 00:02:08,870
So they brought in Chinese and Persian engineers.

54
00:02:08,950 --> 00:02:10,295
Soon they had catapults,

55
00:02:10,315 --> 00:02:12,140
and even gunpowder bombs.

56
00:02:13,150 --> 00:02:15,230
And five. The dark one:

57
00:02:15,390 --> 00:02:16,390
fear.

58
00:02:16,490 --> 00:02:19,320
A city that opened its gates was usually spared.

59
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:21,800
A city that fought back could be wiped out.

60
00:02:21,980 --> 00:02:26,250
In 1221, great cities like Merv and Nishapur were destroyed.

61
00:02:26,350 --> 00:02:28,880
Millions of people died in the Mongol wars.

62
00:02:29,010 --> 00:02:30,687
The stories ran ahead of the army,

63
00:02:30,707 --> 00:02:32,550
and many cities simply gave up.

64
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:35,750
Genghis died in 1227,

65
00:02:35,770 --> 00:02:38,030
but his sons and grandsons kept going.

66
00:02:38,050 --> 00:02:42,050
In 1241, Mongol armies crushed two European armies,

67
00:02:42,070 --> 00:02:44,890
in Poland and Hungary, in the same week.

68
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:46,740
Europe was in panic.

69
00:02:47,470 --> 00:02:49,730
Then the Mongols turned around and left.

70
00:02:49,890 --> 00:02:52,160
Their Great Khan, Ögedei, had died,

71
00:02:52,180 --> 00:02:54,410
and the leaders rode home to choose a new one.

72
00:02:55,430 --> 00:02:58,180
Inside the empire, something surprising happened.

73
00:02:58,460 --> 00:03:01,490
The Mongols mostly did not care about your religion.

74
00:03:01,710 --> 00:03:05,180
Buddhists, Muslims, and Christians all served the khan.

75
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:06,780
Trade was protected.

76
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:08,960
With a metal pass called a paiza,

77
00:03:08,980 --> 00:03:11,140
a traveler could cross all of Asia.

78
00:03:11,370 --> 00:03:13,920
Historians call this the Pax Mongolica:

79
00:03:14,030 --> 00:03:15,260
the Mongol peace.

80
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:18,420
A young man from Venice, Marco Polo,

81
00:03:18,440 --> 00:03:19,525
went all the way to China

82
00:03:19,545 --> 00:03:22,540
and worked for Kublai Khan, Genghis's grandson.

83
00:03:22,930 --> 00:03:26,390
In 1279, Kublai finished the conquest of China.

84
00:03:26,470 --> 00:03:28,230
The crown had a new owner.

85
00:03:28,480 --> 00:03:31,620
The empire now stretched from Korea to Eastern Europe:

86
00:03:31,730 --> 00:03:34,430
about 24 million square kilometers.

87
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:36,260
Almost the size of North America.

88
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:39,520
But even the Mongols had limits.

89
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:42,216
In 1260, the Mamluks of Egypt beat them

90
00:03:42,236 --> 00:03:44,070
at a place called Ain Jalut.

91
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:46,654
And twice, Mongol fleets sailing to Japan

92
00:03:46,674 --> 00:03:48,240
were wrecked by storms.

93
00:03:48,430 --> 00:03:50,520
The Japanese called them kamikaze:

94
00:03:50,540 --> 00:03:51,890
the divine wind.

95
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:55,020
The biggest danger, though, was inside.

96
00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:57,680
Genghis's grandsons fought over the throne,

97
00:03:57,700 --> 00:04:00,900
and by the 1260s the empire had split into four.

98
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:02,750
Each part went its own way.

99
00:04:03,030 --> 00:04:04,295
Less than a hundred years later,

100
00:04:04,315 --> 00:04:06,420
the Mongols were pushed out of China.

101
00:04:07,860 --> 00:04:10,690
So why did the Mongols rule the 1200s?

102
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:13,210
Not because they were rich, or many.

103
00:04:13,410 --> 00:04:14,590
They were faster.

104
00:04:14,630 --> 00:04:16,050
They were better organized.

105
00:04:16,070 --> 00:04:17,610
They learned from everyone.

106
00:04:17,630 --> 00:04:19,900
And they used fear as a weapon.

107
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:24,660
But the open roads that carried silk, spices, and ideas

108
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:26,480
would soon carry something else.

109
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:30,070
In the 1340s, a disease moved west along them:

110
00:04:30,170 --> 00:04:31,270
the Black Death.

111
00:04:32,140 --> 00:04:34,480
But that's the story of the next century.

112
00:04:35,640 --> 00:04:36,737
Not bad, by the way,

113
00:04:36,757 --> 00:04:40,120
for a boy whose family once lost eight of its nine horses.

